    id: "who-we-are",
    heading: "Who we are, and how remote delivery from India works",
    paragraphs: [
      "BtechWaleTech is three engineers based in India. Ankur Kumar is a full stack developer who builds the front ends, back ends and integrations. Santosh Sharma works on AI and machine learning, AWS, data, technical SEO and deployment. Vedansh Shrivastava runs project delivery and works on data science, AI and automation. When a Singapore client messages us, one of those three people replies, and the same people write the code. There is no account manager in between and no hand-off to a subcontractor you never meet.",
      "We are three freelance engineers working from India. We do not have an office in Singapore, a registered entity there, local staff or a Google Business Profile with a Singapore address. Some offshore providers blur this by listing a virtual office or a co-working desk in Raffles Place; we would rather be plain about it. You are hiring a remote team in India, and the rest of this page explains how that works in practice and where it helps or does not help your business.",
      "The practical side is simple. A first conversation happens on WhatsApp or a video call. We write down what you need as a scope document: pages, features, integrations, who supplies content, what is excluded, and a timeline. You approve it before anything is billed. During the build you get a private staging link that you can open on your own phone at any time, so you review real pages rather than static mock-ups. Feedback goes into a shared list, and small changes usually land the same day because our working hours cover your afternoon and evening.",
      "Remote work suits most Singapore projects better than people expect. Singapore businesses are used to working with regional teams in Kuala Lumpur, Manila, Jakarta or Bangalore, and many already run their operations on Google Workspace or Microsoft 365, Slack or WhatsApp groups, and shared documents. What matters is that decisions are written down and that the people doing the work are reachable. We keep everything in writing, answer seven days a week, and never ask you to chase a sales contact for technical answers.",
      "Where remote delivery is weaker, we say so. We cannot visit your shop to photograph products, attend a meeting at your office, or sit with your staff while they learn a new admin panel. For photography we recommend a local photographer and we tell you exactly which shots and sizes we need. For training we record short screen videos you can keep and share with new staff, which tends to outlast any single in-person session.",
