      "Search in Singapore is dominated by Google, and competitive commercial terms are crowded with well-funded agencies, directories and marketplaces. Winning is less about tricks and more about building pages that answer specific questions better than anyone else, on a site that loads fast and is technically clean. That is the part we are good at, and it is the part many Singapore sites neglect.",
      "<strong>Technical foundations.</strong> Every site we build ships with clean URLs, unique titles and descriptions, structured data for your organisation, services, products and FAQs, an XML sitemap, a sensible robots.txt, correct canonical tags and redirects from any old URLs. We connect Google Search Console and check that pages are actually indexed, not just published. For redesigns we map every URL that currently receives traffic and redirect it properly, because most ranking losses after a Singapore site revamp come from broken URLs rather than design.",
      "<strong>Local and neighbourhood search.</strong> Many Singapore searches carry an area: aircon servicing in Tampines, physiotherapy near Novena, tuition in Bishan, a renovation contractor in Punggol. If you genuinely serve those areas, pages that describe your service there with real detail, such as travel times, parking or MRT access, and the jobs you do there, can rank well. Thin pages that only swap the area name do not, and can harm the whole site. Your Google Business Profile must be owned and verified by you at your real address; we guide you on categories, services and posts, but we never create or manage profiles at addresses you do not operate from.",
      "<strong>English, Chinese, Malay and Tamil.</strong> Singapore has four official languages, and the right language mix depends on your customers. A B2B software firm may need only English. A traditional Chinese medicine clinic, a bak kwa brand or a property agent serving older buyers may need Simplified Chinese pages too. A halal food business or a Malay wedding vendor may benefit from Malay content. We set up separate URLs for each language, a visible language switcher, hreflang tags such as en-SG, zh-SG and ms-SG pointing between equivalent pages, and fonts that render Chinese characters cleanly without slowing the page. Machine translation alone rarely reads naturally, so the translated text should be written or reviewed by you or a translator you trust.",
