  {
    id: "compliance",
    heading: "PDPA, the Spam Control Act and GST: what your website must handle",
    paragraphs: [
      "This section describes the practical groundwork we build into Singapore websites. It is not legal or tax advice. We are engineers, not lawyers, and for formal questions you should speak to a qualified adviser in Singapore.",
      "<strong>Personal Data Protection Act (PDPA).</strong> The PDPA governs how organisations in Singapore collect, use and disclose personal data, and the Personal Data Protection Commission enforces it. For a typical website that means contact and enquiry forms, newsletter signups, account registration, checkout, booking systems, analytics, chat widgets and marketing pixels. We build forms that state clearly why each piece of information is collected, ask for consent without pre-ticked boxes, collect only the fields you actually need, and link to a privacy policy that names how to contact your data protection officer. Organisations are expected to designate a DPO and make their business contact information available; we add that contact to the site so people can reach them.",
      "<strong>Protecting what you collect.</strong> Form submissions go into access-controlled storage, not a public spreadsheet link. Admin panels use strong authentication and role-based access, so a part-time staff member sees only what they need. Backups are encrypted, and we avoid copying production customer data onto developer machines; for testing we use dummy data wherever possible. Where a system does hold significant personal data, we help you document where data lives and who can access it, which makes breach assessment and any required notification far easier if something goes wrong.",
      "<strong>Do Not Call and marketing messages.</strong> The PDPA's Do Not Call provisions cover marketing messages sent to Singapore telephone numbers by call, SMS or messaging apps. If your website feeds leads into WhatsApp or SMS marketing, the workflow needs consent records and a way to check numbers against the DNC Registry where the rules require it. We build the plumbing, such as consent capture with timestamps and opt-out handling; the compliance decisions remain yours.",
      "<strong>Spam Control Act.</strong> The Spam Control Act regulates unsolicited commercial electronic messages sent in bulk, including email. Among other things, such messages need an unsubscribe facility that works, accurate sender information, and an advertisement label in the subject line where the message is unsolicited. For newsletters and automated email sequences we set up providers that handle unsubscribe links and suppression lists properly, and we recommend confirmed opt-in for mailing lists so your list is clean from the start.",
      "<strong>GST.</strong> Singapore's goods and services tax is currently 9 percent. Businesses above the registration threshold must register, and registered businesses need GST-correct invoices and price displays. For online stores we configure tax settings, GST-inclusive consumer prices and invoice templates to match your accountant's instructions. On our own invoices: we are an India-based provider and are not GST-registered in Singapore, so we do not add Singapore GST. Whether the reverse charge applies to services you import depends on your company's GST position; your accountant will know.",
      "<strong>Accessibility.</strong> Singapore does not have a single accessibility law for private websites comparable to some other countries, but accessible sites are simply better for everyone, including older customers and anyone on a small phone in bright sunlight. We build to WCAG 2.2 AA as a working standard: sufficient colour contrast, keyboard navigation, meaningful link text, alt text on images, labelled form fields and readable font sizes.",
